## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization, race conditions with thread lifecycle, and memory-ordering guarantees in kernel-space futex design.

## Key Concepts
- Futex fast vs slow path
- Per-address wait queues
- Robust futex list and exit handling
- Race conditions during thread termination
- Memory barriers and wake ordering

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of fast-path vs slow-path for futex_wait
typedef struct { atomic_int *addr; wait_queue_t *wq; robust_list_t *robust; } futex_t;
int futex_wait(int *addr, int expected) {
  // Fast path: if *addr != expected, return immediately
  if (atomic_load(addr) != expected) return EAGAIN;

  // Slow path: enqueue and block
  futex_t f = {addr, get_wait_queue(addr), current->robust_list};
  add_to_robust_list(&f);
  smp_mb(); // Memory barrier before sleep
  return wait_event_interruptible(f.wq, atomic_load(addr) != expected);
}
```

## Test Matrix
| Race Condition | Expected Behavior |
|----------------|------------------|
| Thread exits during wait | Robust list cleanup wakes waiters |
| Wake before wait | Waiter sees updated value, returns immediately |
| Requeue race | Waiter moves to new queue without lost wakeups |

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical understanding of timer-driven thread wakeups, race condition handling, and efficient data structures in a kernel-like environment. It tests a candidate's ability to design a concrete mechanism that works correctly under contention while maintaining precise timing guarantees.

## Key Concepts

- Sleep queues and timer interrupt handling
- Min-heap data structure for efficient wake-time ordering
- Concurrency safety and time rechecking to mitigate timer jitter
- Correctness guarantees: ensuring no missed wakeups and exact timing

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal sketch of a per-CPU sleep queue
typedef struct SleepEntry {
  uint64_t wake_at;
  int tid;
  struct SleepEntry *next;
} SleepEntry;

// Enqueue thread for timed sleep
void sleep(int tid, uint64_t duration) {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  SleepEntry entry = {now + duration, tid};
  minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
  block_thread(tid);
}

// Timer interrupt handler
void timer_interrupt() {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  while (!minheap_empty(&cpu->sleep_queue)) {
    SleepEntry entry = minheap_peek(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at > now) break;

    entry = minheap_pop(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at <= now) {
      wake_thread(entry.tid);
    } else {
      minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
    }
  }
}
```

